  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024-25, the Australia-PNG Subnational Program (APSP) provided technical advisory support to the National Economic and Fiscal Commission (NEFC) for its Intergovernmental Financing Arrangement Review (IGFAR). This comprehensive review included an analysis of costing models and benchmarks across Papua New Guinea and provided recommendations in support of PNG&#x2019;s decentralisation of government reforms across five key areas:
&#x200d;
   -Fiscal Decentralisation and Revenue Mobilisation.
&#x200d;
   -Integrated Planning and Budgeting.
&#x200d;
   -Public Finance Management Systems.
&#x200d;
   -Policy, Institutional and Legislative Frameworks; and
&#x200d;
   -Monitoring and Reporting
&#x200d;
Technical assistance was extended to NEFC to map the process and review provincial 2018-2024 expenditure reports to enhance performance and accountability at the subnational level. An Australian-funded technical advisor is currently supporting the 5-yearly comprehensive survey assessing the cost of delivering essential government services at the provincial level. The technical advisor also provided technical in-house capacity development for NEFC staff. The APSP continues to offer technical assistance to NEFC to draft a technical discussion paper outlining the future direction of the IGFAR and develop an intergovernmental fiscal strategy.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Inadequate resources, capacity and governance undermine subnational service delivery, including access to safe drinking water. PNG&#x2019;s rural population overwhelmingly relies on women and girls to collect water from natural sources vulnerable to climate change. In 2023-24, the Australia-supported Subnational Governance Implementation Plan (SGIP) partnered with three provincial governments to strengthen water security in rural communities by; planning and commencing installation of 100 rainwater tanks across East Sepik, Morobe and Western Highlands provinces; establishing provincial &#x2018;water security governance committees&#x2019; that prioritised user-groups to inform site selection; ensuring active committee representation from women and people with disabilities, voicing needs of primary user-groups; agreeing installation at non-urban small markets and schools, as community sites most frequented by women, children, people with disabilities and other vulnerable user-groups; and, attracting financial co-contributions from two provinces, reflecting local commitment and investment in this initiative.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Organisational Capacity Assessments (OCA) were jointly conducted with the provincial governments in Morobe, Western, Western Highlands, and West Sepik Provinces, in partnership with PNG&#x2019;s Department of Provincial and Local Level Government Affairs and National Economic and Fiscal Commission. The OCAs reviewed the capacity and capabilities of participating provincial administrations, identified strengths and weaknesses in their abilities to perform against corporate mandates and service delivery responsibilities in their respective Districts and Local Level Government areas. Next steps will include recommendations for identified areas of support to strengthen the administrations&#x2019; capacity to implement their service delivery mandates.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australia-PNG Subnational Program (APSP) partnered with the East Sepik, Morobe and Western Highlands Provincial Governments to strengthen water security and improve access to basic drinking water in rural communities. Across 2024-25, Australia supported the construction of 43 water security projects (rainwater tanks), at 15 rural markets in East Sepik, 23 elementary schools in Morobe, and 5 rural markets in Western Highlands. This brings total construction since 2023-24 to 99 rainwater tanks across 59 sites in these three provinces.
&#x200d;
Morobe Province: 40 structures at 31 elementary schools, supporting early childhood health and hygiene, and improving classroom and learning conditions for over 8000 students and teachers. 
&#x200d;
East Sepik Province: 30 structures at 15 rural markets, improving sanitation and water access, public health and economic resilience for vendors (predominantly women).
&#x200d;
Western Highlands Province: 29 structures at 13 rural markets, improving sanitation and water access, public health and economic resilience for vendors (predominantly women).
&#x200d;
Crucially, local leadership and inclusive decision-making processes meant the selected sites maximised community impact by prioritising access for  women, children, people with disabilities and other vulnerable user-groups. 
Climate risks, adaption and public health considerations were embedded from planning to final design, ensuring durable, sustainable and climate-resilient infrastructure.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">To support PNG&#x2019;s decentralised system of government, the Australia-PNG Subnational Program (APSP) supports provincial administrations to lift their governance and coordination capacity. By facilitating Organisational Capacity Assessments and targeted technical advisory support in several provinces, including Morobe, East Sepik, West Sepik and Western Highlands, we assist subnational governments improve governance, accountability, service delivery and development outcomes at local levels. 
&#x200d;
For example, in 2024-25, this support assisted the Morobe Provincial Administration (MPA) to convene two Provincial Development Partners Coordination Forums, bringing together representatives from government, private-sector, non-government and civil society organisations. The Forums foster direct dialogue with the MPA about the alignment, coordination and management of development assistance. Our support also enabled the MPA to revitalise their Provincial Coordination and Monitoring Committee &#x2013; a mandated governance arrangement. These efforts enabled the MPA to meaningfully engage at provincial, district and national levels to coordinate, monitor and assess the effectiveness of policy implementation and service delivery within the province.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
